CNS — HEAD, BRAIN, CONTROL PLANE & SETTINGS ARCHITECTURE

Status: FINAL ARCHITECTURE DIRECTION

Scope: CNS control plane, chat control, brain/model orchestration, memory continuity, bots, connections, extensions and settings

> Important: This document defines the architecture and responsibilities of the CNS control plane.
It does not freeze every internal setting of every future subsystem. When a subsystem such as Bots, Memory, Trading or Brain is implemented, its own detailed design chapter must expand the relevant section without breaking this architecture.

Do not invent unnecessary settings merely to fill the UI. Build the actual settings required by the subsystem when its implementation is designed.




---

1. FUNDAMENTAL PRINCIPLE

CNS and an LLM are not the same thing.

CNS is the system.

LLMs are interchangeable reasoning resources used by CNS.

CNS
                          │
              ┌───────────┴───────────┐
              │                       │
        CONTROL PLANE             CNS CORE
              │                       │
       Brain / Memory /          Trading / State /
       Tools / Connections       Workflows / Knowledge
              │
        ┌─────┼─────┐
        │     │     │
      Local  Remote Paid
      Models Models Models

The model can change.

CNS identity, memory, projects, workflows, permissions and system state must remain.


---

2. THE CNS CONTROL SURFACES

CNS must be controllable through multiple interfaces:

USER
                   │
       ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       │           │           │
      CHAT        GUI         CLI
       │           │           │
       └───────────┼───────────┘
                   │
               CNS CONTROL
                   │
          ┌────────┼────────┐
          │        │        │
       Agents  Automation  System

Voice can operate through the Chat interface.

Agents and automation can also use the same underlying control interfaces.

Critical rule

Chat, GUI and CLI must not become separate implementations of CNS logic.

They should ultimately communicate with the same CNS control APIs/interfaces.


---

3. CHAT IS THE PRIMARY COCKPIT

The user should not have to leave the conversation every time they want to activate a capability.

The chat interface should provide compact controls for relevant capabilities.

Conceptually:

[ + ] [ 🧠 Model ] [ Think ] [ Research ] [ 🌐 ] [ 🎙 ] [ 📹 ] [ Send ]

The exact UI can evolve.

The principle is:

> Frequently used capabilities should be accessible directly from the conversation.



Examples:

Select/change brain

Thinking mode

Research mode

Internet

Voice

Video

Attachments

Other model-supported capabilities


Advanced configuration remains inside Settings.


---

4. CHAT MODEL BAR

The user should be able to see and control the current brain/model from the conversation.

For example:

🧠 Qwen 8B Q4
LOCAL • CNS SERVER • READY

or:

🧠 GPT ...
AUTHORIZED SUBSCRIPTION
REASONING • READY

The model display should expose useful information such as:

Model family

Model/version

Local/remote

Provider or compute home

Quantization/size where applicable

Important capabilities

Current availability/status


This prevents confusion between a full hosted model and a smaller quantized local model.


---

5. AUTO BRAIN MODE

CNS should have an AUTO mode.

In AUTO mode CNS evaluates the task and chooses an appropriate available reasoning resource according to:

Task complexity

Required capabilities

Model capability

Context requirements

Availability

Local resources

Provider health

Cost policy

User preferences

Privacy policy

Current model state


The user must always be able to override AUTO.

Examples:

> “Use local DeepSeek.”



> “Use Qwen.”



> “Use only free models.”



> “Don't use remote models.”



> “Use my authorized paid model.”



> “Use the strongest available model.”




---

6. CNS MUST NOT SILENTLY MAKE EXPENSIVE DECISIONS

If a task appears substantially more demanding than the current model can reasonably handle, CNS may recommend escalation.

Example:

> ⚡ This task requires stronger reasoning.
Current brain: Local Qwen 8B
Recommended: Authorized GPT
Use stronger brain?



The user decides according to their configured policy.

CNS may also have an automatic policy such as:

Allow paid model automatically: NO
Ask before paid usage: YES
Maximum permitted cost: configured by user

The exact cost controls will be designed later.


---

7. “WHY DID CNS CHOOSE THIS MODEL?”

When AUTO selects a model, the user should optionally be able to inspect the decision.

Example:

Why this model?

Task:
Complex system architecture

Requirements:
High reasoning
Long context
Technical synthesis

Selected:
Authorized GPT

Reason:
Required capability unavailable locally.
Free remote models currently unavailable.
User policy permits authorized paid model.

This creates transparency without forcing the user to understand the internal routing system.


---

8. THE BRAIN IS A ROUTING SYSTEM, NOT ONE MODEL

CNS must support multiple directions of model supply.

Path A — Same model, different home

Qwen
 ↓
Home A unavailable
 ↓
Home B
 ↓
Local Qwen

Path B — Same family, different model

Qwen 30B
 ↓
Unavailable
 ↓
Qwen 14B
 ↓
Qwen 8B

Path C — Different model family

Qwen
 ↓
Unavailable / unsuitable
 ↓
DeepSeek
 ↓
Other approved model

Path D — Premium route

Free/local supply exhausted
             ↓
Authorized subscription / API
             ↓
Stronger model

CNS should therefore think in terms of:

> Model + Capability + Home + Availability + Policy



rather than simply:

> “Which model name?”




---

9. MODEL HOME

A model and its compute home are separate concepts.

For example:

MODEL:
Qwen

HOME:
Local CNS Server

or:

MODEL:
Qwen

HOME:
Remote Provider A

or:

MODEL:
Qwen

HOME:
Remote Provider B

CNS can therefore move between homes without redesigning the model architecture.

Provider health, quota, rate limits, latency and availability can influence routing.


---

10. BRAIN CAPABILITY PIPELINE

This is one of the central CNS mechanisms.

If the user activates:

> Deep Thinking



CNS must not blindly pretend that every model supports it.

Instead:

USER ACTIVATES CAPABILITY
          │
          ▼
   CNS CAPABILITY LAYER
          │
          ▼
CHECK CURRENT MODEL
          │
    ┌─────┴─────┐
    │           │
 SUPPORTED   UNSUPPORTED
    │           │
    ▼           ▼
Activate      Explain /
native        recommend
capability    compatible option

The same architecture applies to:

Deep Thinking

Research

Vision

Audio

Video

Tool calling

Other future capabilities


Where the selected model/provider exposes a native capability, CNS should activate it through the appropriate adapter.

Where it does not, CNS must clearly indicate that.


